Requirements: 6   with obligations: 6

[task-01] The benchmark's command-line runs — the instability measurement and the mutant
    audit — report what they spent when they finish, as `acceptance check` and
    `acceptance decompose` already do. They print the same per-stage usage table, with
    live and replayed calls counted apart, so a large offline run shows what it cost.
    -> benchmark-cli-runs-report-spend-on-finish  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Each of the benchmark's command-line runs, the instability measurement and the
       mutant audit, reports what it spent when it finishes.
    -> cli-runs-print-per-stage-usage-table  [explanation_observability/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The benchmark's command-line runs (the instability measurement and the mutant
       audit) print, when they finish, the same per-stage usage table that `acceptance
       check` and `acceptance decompose` print.
    -> usage-table-separates-live-and-replayed-calls  [explanation_observability/explicit, code_and_tests]
       In the per-stage usage table printed by the benchmark command-line runs, live
       model calls and replayed calls are counted apart, so that each stage's live and
       replayed counts are reported separately rather than combined into a single
       figure.

[task-02] The table shows what the run spent on Claude models separately from what it
    spent on any other provider, so the Claude credits a run consumed can be read off as
    a figure of its own. A call sent through the Message Batches API counts at the price
    it was billed at.
    -> claude-spend-separate-figure  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The table presents the run's spend on Claude models as a figure separate from the
       run's spend on any other provider, so the Claude credits consumed by a run can be
       read off as its own figure.
    -> batch-call-counted-at-billed-price  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A call sent through the Message Batches API is counted in the run's spend at the
       price it was billed at (the batch price), not at the standard non-batch price for
       the same model.

